course: dictionary
lesson: 9670
title: Succeed: try, try again
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To succeed means to do what you tried or hoped to do. «If at first you don't succeed, try, try again» tells you that a first try may not work, but you can keep going until it does.
explain: The context shows you the meaning. «Tigers stalk slowly and quietly, then make a short rush, perhaps only about 1 hunt in 10 succeeds.» A hunt succeeds when the tiger catches its food, so most hunts do not work out.
explain: Succeed can also mean to come after someone and take their place. In history, «a prince succeeds his father as king» means the prince becomes king after his father. To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence: does it still make sense?
fact: Succeed means to do what you tried or hoped to do.
fact: Succeed is a verb, so it changes: succeed, succeeds, succeeded, succeeding.
fact: Common phrases are «succeed in doing something» and «succeed at» an activity.
fact: To succeed someone means to come after them and take their place, like «succeed to the throne».
fact: The opposite of succeed is fail: «A French attempt to dig a canal in Panama failed» and the Americans later succeeded.
exercise: «A "1 in 100" chance means that over very many tries about 1 in 100 succeed.» What does succeed mean here? | Fall over | Stop trying | Sing loudly | *Do what you tried to do
exercise: «The Americans later succeeded» in Panama, after the French had failed. What does succeeded mean? | They gave up on the canal | They started digging a new river | They went home to rest | *They did what they set out to do
exercise: «A prince succeeds his father as king.» What does succeeds mean here? | *Comes after and takes the place of | Forgets about | Argues with | Copies
test: The teacher says, «If at first you don't succeed, try, try again.» What is the teacher encouraging? | Watching the others | Sleeping in | *Trying again after a first failure | Giving up after one try
test: «Perhaps only about 1 hunt in 10 succeeds.» What does this tell you about tigers? | Tigers always catch their food | Tigers never hunt | *Most of their hunts do not work out | Tigers run for hours every day
exam: Which sentence uses succeed to mean take someone's place? | *The prince will succeed his father as king | Ben hopes to succeed in his maths test | Ruth tried to succeed at the high jump | Mia will succeed if she keeps practising
